  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts for the portal scene, and it passes the typecheck. I couldn't run it or build it, so the look, light intensities and frame rate are untested.\n\n- **Portal and ruin:** A stone ring stands on a two-tier platform with a plinth, with a glowing inner rim and 24 rune lights on the front and back faces. Seven pillars (some broken) surround it, with scattered rubble and ground slabs. The scene has dusk lighting, haze, a dark sky gradient, bloom and a vignette.\n- **Disc:** A custom shader draws a rotating spiral vortex in cyan and violet with a bright core. Two additive halo planes and a glow pool on the platform add the bloom-like glow.\n- **Light pulse:** Three point lights (cyan, violet, blue) pulse slowly on a sine wave, and the shader glow pulses with them.\n- **Particles:** 170 particles spiral inward toward the portal and 80 motes rise from the platform and shrink and dim as they fade. Both sets are updated every frame and recycled when they finish.\n- **Camera:** The camera is fixed. The whole ruin sways about ±24° around the portal axis instead of orbiting, so the vortex is never seen edge-on.\n- **Task state:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated every frame with `portalActive: true`, the live particle count and the elapsed time. `__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()`.\n\nI guessed the point-light intensities (about 15–50) from the docs, and `material.emissive` for the particles with instance colors applied to both base and emissive. If the particles come out plain white or the stones look too dark or too bright, those are the first things to adjust.",
